// fx sqrt -- exact integer square root, bit by bit.
//
// There is no sqrt builtin, so this is the digit-by-digit method in base 4: try
// to set one bit of the result at a time, highest first, keeping the remaining
// part of the radicand. It is exact -- the floor of the true root for every a in
// 0..2^31-1 -- and it never overflows, because the largest quantity it forms is
// result+bit, which peaks at 1342177280 (verified by exhaustive simulation) well
// inside int range.
//
// Newton's method would be shorter but needs a division per step and a starting
// guess, and its final "did I land one below?" fixup is the same amount of code
// as this whole loop. A negative a returns 0 rather than aborting.
//
// **fx_sqrt is silently wrong above 2^31** -- fx_sqbit cannot start high enough
// and the root comes back far too small with no fault raised. Go through fx_hyp
// or fx_wroot, which recurse down into this domain, rather than calling it with
// a wide value.
//
// The running state is a 2-slot list `sq`: 0 = the part of a not yet accounted
// for, 1 = the root built so far (doubled, which is what the base-4 recurrence
// carries). A list is used rather than three threaded parameters so the caller
// keeps its own action budget.
//
// Lifted from idem/engine/core/math/fx/wide/sqrt.id.

fx_sqrt(int a) {
  int[] sq = [a, 0];
  int m = 0;
  if (a > 0) {
    int fx_sqbit_v = fx_sqbit(a);
    m = fx_sqloop(sq, fx_sqbit_v);
  }
} return int m;
(16):(4)
(0):(0)
(-5):(0)
(2147483647):(46340)

// The highest power of 4 that is not greater than a. 1073741824 is 4^15, the
// largest that fits an int, so the walk down is at most 15 steps.
fx_sqbit(int a) {
  int bt = 1073741824;
  while (bt > a) {
    bt = bt / 4;
  }
} return int bt;
(0):(0)
(5):(4)
(1073741824):(1073741824)

fx_sqloop(int[] sq, int bt) {
  while (bt > 0) {
    fx_sqstep(sq, bt);
    bt = bt / 4;
  }
  int ret_i = sq[1];
} return int ret_i;
([16, 0], 16):(4)
([1000000, 0], 262144):(1000)
